---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "about", label: "About" },
  { id: "opacities", label: "Opacities" },
  { id: "overriding", label: "Overriding" },
  { id: "shapes", label: "Two shapes" },
  { id: "focus-indicator", label: "Focus indicator" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="state_layers" sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.about}>
    <p class="flow-text">
      A state layer is the translucent wash a component paints over itself to show that you are
      hovering it, that it has focus, or that you are pressing it. Material 3 uses it in place of
      the ink ripple. It is a <em>foundation</em>, so you never write markup for one. Components
      paint it behind themselves, and these tokens decide how strong it is.
    </p>
    <p>
      The opacity lives in one place, so changing it at runtime updates every component at once
      without a rebuild.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.opacities}>
    <table class="striped">
      <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Value</th><th>Applies when</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--md-sys-state-hover-state-layer-opacity</code></td><td>0.08</td><td>The pointer is over the control.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--md-sys-state-focus-state-layer-opacity</code></td><td>0.1</td><td>The control has visible focus.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--md-sys-state-pressed-state-layer-opacity</code></td><td>0.1</td><td>The control is being pressed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--md-sys-state-dragged-state-layer-opacity</code></td><td>0.16</td><td>The control is being dragged.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      These are the <code>md.sys.state</code> values from the Material 3 token set. Focus and
      pressed are both <code>0.1</code>.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.overriding}>
    <p>
      Set the token on <code>:root</code> to change every component, or on a subtree to change
      only what is inside it.
    </p>
<Code code={`
<style>
  :root { --md-sys-state-hover-state-layer-opacity: 0.12; }
</style>
`} />
    <p>
      Some components have their own state layer token. Set it to change one component without
      touching the rest. Its default value still comes from the foundation token.
    </p>
<Code code={`
<style>
  .my-panel { --md-comp-card-hover-state-layer-opacity: 0.04; }
</style>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.shapes}>
    <p>
      Most components draw the layer as an overlay filling the control. Checkboxes and radio
      buttons have no room for one, so they draw it as a ring growing out from the control's edge
      to the state layer size. Both read the same opacity, so they stay in step.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S["focus-indicator"]}>
    <p>
      Keyboard focus also draws a ring outside the control, in
      <code>--md-sys-color-secondary</code>. Its size comes from two more <code>md.sys.state</code>
      tokens, and every 3px component focus ring reads them. Put <code>.focus-ring</code> on an
      app's own focusable element to give it the same ring. Text fields and selects mark focus with
      their own 2px <code>primary</code> outline instead.
    </p>
    <table class="striped">
      <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Value</th><th>Sets</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--md-sys-state-focus-indicator-thickness</code></td><td>3px</td><td>The ring's width.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--md-sys-state-focus-indicator-outer-offset</code></td><td>2px</td><td>The gap between the element and the ring.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
<Code code={`
<style>
  :root { --md-sys-state-focus-indicator-thickness: 4px; }
</style>
<div class="focus-ring" tabindex="0">Drop files here</div>
`} />
    <p>
      A few rows inside scrolling lists draw the ring inside their edge instead, so the list does
      not clip it. Those keep their own inset and still take the width from the token.
    </p>
  </Section>
</DocsLayout>
